WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.680
Today, we're back with unintended consequences, Episode 39 for June 24th, 2026.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Every story we cover starts with someone who had a reasonable plan.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.760
In the spring of 1968, a Boston gynecologist examined a cluster of young women.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:23.040
Some still in their teens, the patients arrive with the diagnosis of clear cell,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:29.640
addo dechromascona of the vagina, a form of cancer that develops in the tissue lining the

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:34.360
vaginal canal. That particular cancer was so uncommon in their age group

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:40.020
that the hospital's pathology department had seen only one prior case in 30 years.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.560
Each of the patient's mothers had taken the same medication during pregnancy.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.660
The medication was diethyl still bestraw or DES.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.220
The drug had been introduced with the explicit goal of supporting pregnancies that might

00:00:55.220 --> 00:01:01.700
otherwise end in miscarriage. Instead, it produced a cancer that would not appear until the exposed

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:09.080
daughters reached adolescence or early adulthood. The compound was first synthesized in 1938 by

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:16.040
British biochemist Sir Charles Dodds and his team at the Courtold Institute. Dodds was seeking

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:21.900
an inexpensive orally active estrogen that could treat menopausal symptoms without relying on

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:28.860
scarce natural extracts from animal ovaries. Within a few years, American obstetricians began

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:35.120
testing the same molecule for a different purpose. They wanted to address recurrent miscarriage

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:42.100
and preterm labor. At the time, miscarriage was understood largely as a failure of hormonal support.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:48.940
Estrogen levels were observed to drop before many spontaneous abortions. Researchers at Harvard

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:54.620
and the University of Chicago, therefore reasoned that administering a potent synthetic estrogen,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.920
might restore the hormonal environment required to maintain pregnancy.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.520
Early case series published in the American Journal of Obstetrics and Gynecology

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:10.880
appear to show reduced rates of loss. The logic seemed sound given the limited tools available

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:19.600
to physicians in the 1940s. By 1947, the FDA had already approved DES for menopausal symptoms.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:27.180
Its use in pregnancy spread rapidly through off-label prescribing. Pharmaceutical companies marketed it

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:35.020
under more than 200 brand names. By the mid-1950s, an estimated 5-10 million women worldwide had

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:41.240
received the drug during gestation. That range, 5 million on the lower end and 10 million on the

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:46.940
higher end, reflects the rough counts compiled later from prescription records and marketing data

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:52.040
across multiple countries. Professional journals carried advertisements claiming it produced

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:58.100
stronger healthier babies. A small number of researchers, including British pharmacologists

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:04.380
or Robert Robinson, had noted in the early 1940s that high-dose estrogens could alter

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:10.880
genital tract development in animal embryos. Yet, these findings received little clinical attention.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.060
Most practitioners viewed DES as a low-risk intervention that addressed a common and

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:23.380
distressing problem. One might ask why the animal data did not slow adoption, but at the time the

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.360
prevailing view held that findings in non-human embryos did not necessarily translate to human

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:37.200
pregnancy outcomes under medical supervision. In 1971, physicians Arthur Herbst and Robert Scully

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.400
published a case-control study in the New England Journal of Medicine. The study linked

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:50.160
maternal DES use to the rare vaginal cancers appearing in their daughters. The median age at

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:58.340
diagnosis was 19. Subsequent research established that DES crossed the placenta and interfered with

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.640
the developing mylearian ducts. This interference produced permanent structural changes that later

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:11.660
became malignant. The same exposure also increased risks of infertility, ectopic pregnancy,

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:18.380
and breast cancer in the daughters. Later studies documented elevated rates of hypospatius and other

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:24.560
genital anomalies in sons. Because the drug had been given to healthy pregnant women, rather than

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:32.160
to patients already ill, the population exposed in utero was unusually large. The latency period of

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.600
roughly 20 years meant that the connection between prescription and disease was invisible to the

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:43.740
original prescribers and to the women who took the medication. No short-term clinical trial could

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:50.740
have detected an outcome that manifested only after the children reached adulthood. On 22 November

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:59.240
1971, the FDA withdrew approval of DES for pregnancy use. Cancer registries and specialized

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:05.740
clinics were established to follow exposed individuals. Several large lawsuits reached settlement in the

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:13.000
1980s. The drug remained available for other indications for a time, but its obstetric use ended

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:19.860
abruptly. Long-term follow-up has continued into the third generation. Some grandchildren of exposed

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:26.660
women show subtle reproductive tract changes, although cancer risk in that cohort remains understudy.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:33.940
Modern endocrine disruptor research now routinely examines mutile generational effects that were not

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:41.480
considered in mid-century drug evaluation. The DES story illustrates that interventions acting on

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:48.180
developing physiology can produce effects whose full scope appears only after an entire generation has

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:56.120
passed. It also shows that absence of observed harm in short-term studies does not equal proof of

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:03.480
long-term safety when the endpoint lies decades ahead. Finally, the episode underscores the value of

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:09.900
retaining detailed exposure records. Without the maternal medication history is collected by Herbst's

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:16.580
team, the causal link would have remained invisible. When new medical or policy tools are introduced at

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:22.660
scale, what mechanisms exist to detect consequences that surface only in the next generation?

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:28.780
That's unintended consequences for today. If this episode gave you something to think about,

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:34.880
share it with a friend who'd appreciate the story. Subscribe wherever you listen and we'll be back

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:40.820
tomorrow with another case. And before you go, this show is part of the Nair A Network,

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:47.980
a family of daily podcasts covering tech, science, markets, and more. If you enjoyed today's episode,

